Spec: main

Purpose:
  Be the `kubectl-vynil` bin half of the crate: one straight-line entry — rustls `ring`
    provider pin, the exact-argv[1] `__complete` string intercept that hands the raw argv tail
    to @completion::@run before clap ever runs, @Cli::@parse for everything else, a five-arm
    fan-out onto @run_jukebox/@run_instance/@emit_script, and the @anyhow::@Result left to the
    std Termination exit. The file mints no error, writes no byte and calls no
    @std::process::@exit itself; the completion wire, the grammar and its exits, and every
    diagnosed message belong to the specs referenced below.

Owns:
  ./main.rs

References:
  ./cli.sdd — the grammar and its in-@Cli::@parse exit surface: help/version on stdout exit
    `0`, every failure exit `2`, and the absence of `__complete` from that grammar — clap
    rejects it as an unknown subcommand (tip pointing at `completion`) wherever the intercept
    below does not fire.
  ./completion.sdd — @run: the sole `__complete` handler, infallible, always ending its stdout
    with the `:4` directive, silent on cluster failure; @emit_script: the embedded loader print
    behind the parsed `completion` verb. Both land in this file's exit `0`.
  ./actions.sdd — the dispatch targets and the crate's exit posture: @Ok → `0`, @Err → one
    `Error:` stderr record exit `1`, every `SCAN-ERR`/`UPG-ERR`/`DIAG-ERR`/`INST-ERR` id minted
    there, and the @unreachable! panic lane that crosses @block_on as exit `101`; `-n` and
    `--context` defaulting happens in actions, never here.
  ./transport.sdd — the total fetch layer under the dispatched read verbs: no transport failure
    can ever reach this @Result (it folds into status-`0` results ./actions.rs judges), which is
    why the exit map has exactly one error lane.
  ./lib.sdd — pending; the lib root whose `pub mod` surface this bin imports as
    @kubectl_vynil (the `kubectl-vynil` package with hyphens underscored).
  /operator/src/main.sdd, /server/src/main.sdd — sibling bin startups with the same
    @install_default().@ok() ring pin (operator silent, server debug-lined); operator hand-
    builds its tokio runtime while this one trusts the macro — Tasks.

Depends on:
  @tokio `1.52.3` (/kubectl-vynil/Cargo.toml features `full`) through #[tokio::@main] —
    tokio-macros `2.7.0` (/Cargo.lock) supplies the runtime builder, @block_on and the exit
    plumbing.
  @rustls `0.23.41` with the `ring` feature — @install_default pin only; the same crate choice
    behind the pure-rustls build the parent spec freezes.
  @anyhow `1.0.103` — @main's @Result type; std @Termination on @Err is anyhow's @Debug
    rendering, described in Returns.
  @clap `4.6.1` — @Cli::@parse only; that call's exits and texts belong to ./cli.sdd.
  @kube `3.1.0`/k8s-openapi indirectly — reached only through the callees; this file names no
    client type.

Must:
  Carry exactly the crate's standard pair of attributes —
    `#![cfg_attr(not(test), deny(clippy::unwrap_used, clippy::expect_used))]` then
    `#![cfg_attr(not(test), warn(clippy::arithmetic_side_effects, clippy::indexing_slicing))]`
    — and hold no literal @unwrap or @expect site: the only @expect is tokio-macros' own
    expansion, exempt because tokio-macros wraps it in `#[allow(clippy::expect_used)]`.
  Run @main as one pass, no loop or retry, in this order: rustls provider pin → argv intercept
    scan → @Cli::@parse → one match on `&@cli.command` whose winning arm's awaited @Result is
    @main's tail expression. No tracing subscriber, no cluster probe, no signal handling, no
    version/banner line; `__complete` and `completion` are the only lanes that conclude inside
    the file.
  Pin rustls before any argument is read — @rustls::@crypto::@ring::@default_provider then
    @install_default with the @Result dropped by @ok(): installed-first @Ok and already-set
    @Err both silent, the effective provider never inspected, and the completion lane pays the
    pin too (Tasks `[?]` on the three-bin copy).
  Intercept `__complete` before parsing, at the frozen position taken from @std::@env::@args():
    consume-then-discard argv[0] (its value is never used, plugin name included), byte-exact
    @Some(`"__complete"`) comparison on argv[1], case-sensitive. On a match collect the rest of
    argv verbatim — zero words possible, `""` and hyphen words included, no trimming or
    filtering — await @completion::@run(words) and return @Ok(()): clap never runs, so a
    completion tail never meets the grammar or @clap::error::ErrorKind::ArgumentConflict
    (completion.sdd digests it by hand), and exit is `0` whatever @run printed.
  Fall through completely when the comparison fails (any other argv[1], or none): the scan
    iterator is dropped without replaying the consumed words, @Cli::@parse re-reads the original
    process argv, and a `__complete` at any later position is clap's unknown-subcommand problem
    — ./cli.sdd and ./completion.sdd both freeze that positional fragility; this file is the
    sole enforcer of the position.
  Take the runtime from the bare #[tokio::@main] — no flavor, @worker_threads, @thread_name or
    @start_paused knob: tokio-macros `2.7.0` expands it to
    @tokio::@runtime::@Builder::@new_multi_thread().@enable_all().@build().@expect(`"Failed building the Runtime"`).@block_on(body)
    with `return` — multi-thread flavor, tokio's default worker count (available parallelism),
    build failure a panic to exit `101` (Tasks `[?]`), and the compiled surface a sync
    `fn @main() -> @anyhow::Result<@()>` whose @Ok/@Err hit std Termination exactly as a plain
    Rust main's do.
  Fan the five arms verbatim over borrowed args (match on `&@cli.command`, so @run_jukebox takes
    @&@JukeboxArgs and @run_instance @&@InstanceArgs — nothing moved): @Commands::Jukebox →
    @run_jukebox(args, context) and this is the only arm @cli.namespace never reaches (the kind
    is cluster-scoped; Tasks `[?]`); @Commands::Vti → @run_instance(&@TENANT_INSTANCE, args,
    context, namespace), @Commands::Vsvc → @run_instance(&@SERVICE_INSTANCE, args, context,
    namespace), @Commands::Vsi → @run_instance(&@SYSTEM_INSTANCE, args, context, namespace);
    @Commands::Completion → @completion::@emit_script(args.shell) then a literal @Ok(()).
  Pass the two globals as @Option<&@str> through @as_deref, verbatim — no defaulting,
    trimming, validating or env-fallback of `context`/`namespace` anywhere in this file;
    ./actions.sdd owns defaulting and ./transport.sdd owns context resolution.

Must not:
  Dodge the Result-as-exit posture: no @std::process::@exit (not even an immediate `exit(0)`
    after @emit_script), no abort, no @std::process::@abort — the only exits are the returned
    @Result, the ones clap takes inside @Cli::@parse, and panics.
  Replay or forward argv on the completion lane: after `__complete` matched, @Cli::@parse is
    never reached and the scanned words are not re-fed to anything but @completion::@run.

Exposes:
  @main — written `#[tokio::@main] async fn @main() -> @anyhow::Result<@()>`, compiled to the
    sync entry of the `kubectl-vynil` bin target (../Cargo.toml `[[bin]]`) — the same string is
    the installed file name this kubectl plugin ships under, and ./completion.sdd's scripts
    attach to that bare token. A bin is not importable: the whole surface is the
    pin-intercept-dispatch sequence and the exit matrix in Returns.

Accepts:
  Process argv only: argv[0] discarded, argv[1] tested only against `__complete`, and the
    completion tail any @String words. Every other shape — flags, kinds, verbs, positionals,
    `--`, empty values — is @Cli::@parse's acceptance, frozen in ./cli.sdd; unknown arguments
    simply take that lane and exit `2`.
  No stdin, no config file, no flags read outside clap; the file contains no @std::@env::@var
    at all. Its only env influence is indirect on the one @Err lane: `RUST_BACKTRACE` and
    `RUST_LIB_BACKTRACE` decide the backtrace section of anyhow's @Debug print.
  Preconditions it trusts unchecked: callees present as working, the bin invoked with a usable
    argv, and — for the shipped contract — a kubeconfig or in-cluster identity reachable by the
    callees whenever a cluster lane runs.

Returns:
  OS exit codes are the only values leaving the process, via the returned @Result, clap's own
    in-@parse exits, and panics:
  `0` — every @Ok(()): a runner's success verdict unchanged from ./actions.rs, the
    completion-intercept return whatever @run wrote, and the `completion bash|zsh` script lane.
  `1` — @Err(@anyhow::@Error): std prints one @eprintln record `Error: ` + the error's @Debug,
    which anyhow renders as the top @Display text first (typically ./actions.rs' `ID: text`
    @bail/@Context message), then `\n\nCaused by:` with its cause(s) when a @Context wrap
    exists (unnumbered line for a single cause, `    N:` numbering for several), then a
    `Stack backtrace:` section only when `RUST_BACKTRACE`/`RUST_LIB_BACKTRACE` captured one.
  `0` stdout / `2` stderr — the usage surface and the failure surface clap decides inside
    @Cli::@parse, texts owned by ./cli.sdd; neither ever becomes a main @Result.
  `101` — any panic: the ones in Raises.
  The file writes no stdout/stderr of its own: every observable byte is clap's, a callee's, or
    the std/macro exit channels.

Raises:
  Constructs zero errors — no @bail, no @Context, no @panic! text originates here; @Err values
    are callee lanes (./actions.sdd's ids, ./bundle.sdd's pass-throughs) returned untouched.
  Panic lanes, all exit `101` with the panic text on stderr: the macro @expect
    `Failed building the Runtime` when the runtime cannot build (Tasks `[?]`);
    @std::@env::@args()'s per-word invalid-UTF-8 @panic! at whatever word the pre-clap scan
    reaches — always argv[1] (whether or not it completes the intercept), and on a completion
    run every later word too, while a clap-lane invocation never scans past argv[1] and clap
    itself reads @args_os bytes happily (Tasks `[?]`); and downstream unwinds such as
    ./actions.sdd's parked @unreachable! arm.
  @completion::@run and @completion::@emit_script cannot fail (./completion.sdd), @Cli::@parse
    never returns a @clap::Error, and @run_jukebox/@run_instance signal only through the
    @Result above.

Handles:
  Intercept arms: no argv[1] at all (bare bin) or any other word → clap lane with the original
    argv; `__complete` matched with an empty tail → @completion::@run with the empty @Vec, which
    completion.sdd answers as the empty-prefix kind slot + `:4`.
  Provider answers: installed-first @Ok and already-set @Err both dropped by @ok() — no branch
    inspects either.
  `completion` arm: the (unit) @emit_script answer is not consulted; @Ok(()) is manufactured
    after it without touching the cluster.
  `jukebox` globals: a parsed `-n`/`@cli.namespace` is accepted by clap for that verb and
    dropped here — only `--vynil-namespace` reaches the wait loops (./actions.sdd).
  Runner @Err: no catch, transform, retry or extra log — @main's tail hands it to std verbatim.

Tasks:
  [ ] Cover this bin once: no #[cfg(test)] module exists and no test spawns the binary, so all
      six Scenarios rest on reading; a process-level harness must launch the built
      `kubectl-vynil` and assert the exit matrix — `0` on runner-@Ok, intercept and script
      lanes, `1` with the `Error:` first line and its id (e.g. `SCAN-ERR-04`) on an @Err lane,
      `2` on clap rejections, `101` on the invalid-UTF-8 argv lane — and that every byte outside
      clap/callee/std channels is absent.
  [?] The runtime build is the macro's @expect (`Failed building the Runtime`, panic → `101`,
      no way to attach context or take a Result lane without dropping #[tokio::@main]), while
      /operator/src/main.sdd hand-builds its runtime with its own panic text; decide one
      startup-failure posture for the three bins.
  [?] @std::@env::@args() panics on non-UTF-8 words the scan reaches (argv[1] on every lane,
      the whole completion tail) while clap's own @args_os path accepts any bytes — one
      untranslated word turns a normal invocation into exit `101`; decide later whether the
      scan moves to @std::@env::@args_os with lossy/byte passthrough to @completion::@run.
  [?] The exact-argv[1] position is load-bearing and undocumented as a product contract
      (flag-first completion requests die in clap); ./cli.sdd already hosts the replacing-hidden-
      clap-subcommand `[?]` — decide both sides together.
  [?] Silent drop of a parsed `-n` on jukebox commands (global flag accepted, argument
      discarded at the dispatch arm): freeze as intended cluster-scoped behaviour or forward
      something for future use.

Done when:
  Every Scenario maps to a green process-level test or a decided `[?]`, the startup-failure and
    argv-hardening decisions are recorded, and `cargo test -p kubectl-vynil`,
    `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings`, `cargo +nightly fmt --check` and
    `specdd lint` are green.

Scenario: __complete at argv[1] skips clap entirely
  Given `kubectl-vynil __complete vti ''` — the token exactly at argv[1], tail words `vti` and
    the empty word
  When @main runs
  Then @completion::@run receives the two tail words verbatim, stdout is the
    candidate-lines-plus-`:4` wire, and the exit is `0` even with an unreadable kubeconfig or a
    down cluster — @Cli::@parse was never reached, so no clap text and no exit `2` can appear
  No mapped test exists.

Scenario: __complete anywhere else dies in clap
  Given `kubectl-vynil -n ns __complete vti ''` — a floating global in front of the token
  When @main runs
  Then the argv[1] word is `-n`, the comparison fails, the scan is dropped without replay, and
    clap rejects the later `__complete` as an unknown subcommand — exit `2` with ./cli.sdd's
    tip form, not a completion answer
  No mapped test exists.

Scenario: bare or unknown argv takes the clap lane
  Given argv with only the bin name (argv[1] @None ≠ @Some(`"__complete"`)), then `toto` as
    argv[1]
  When @Cli::@parse runs
  Then the bare bin prints the root help to stderr and exits `2`, and `toto` exits `2` with
    `unrecognized subcommand 'toto'` — ./cli.sdd's texts; @main adds no byte and its arms never
    run
  No mapped test exists.

Scenario: a runner Err exits 1 with the diagnosed chain
  Given `box home scan` whose wait loop bails with `SCAN-ERR-04`
  When @main hands that @Err to std
  Then stderr is one record `Error: ` whose first line is the @bail text
    (`SCAN-ERR-04: timed out waiting for job scan-home to be (re)created`), a `Caused by:`
    section only when a @Context wrap exists, a backtrace section only under
    `RUST_BACKTRACE`/`RUST_LIB_BACKTRACE` set to `1`, and the exit status is `1`
  No mapped test exists.

Scenario: the completion verb answers script and zero
  Given `kubectl-vynil completion bash`, then `completion zsh`
  When the @Commands::Completion arm runs
  Then @completion::@emit_script prints the whole embedded loader (clap already restricted the
    shell to @CompletionShell::Bash/@CompletionShell::Zsh), the arm fabricates @Ok(()), and the
    process exits `0` with no cluster traffic of any kind
  No mapped test exists.

Scenario: kind words route to their kind row with globals verbatim
  Given `vsi -n kydah-core reloader agentlog`, and in a parallel run `jukebox home scan`
  When the match arms bind
  Then @run_instance receives &@SYSTEM_INSTANCE, borrowed args, `context` @None and `namespace`
    @Some of the verbatim `kydah-core` — no default happened here — while @run_jukebox receives
    (args, @None) and the parsed namespace is dropped at the arm
  No mapped test exists.

Example:
  intercept consumption: argv `kubectl-vynil __complete vti ''` → @completion::@run gets `vti`
    and `""` in order; argv[0] was consumed, never inspected and never forwarded
  single-cause exit-`1` shape: `Error: SCAN-ERR-04: timed out waiting for job scan-home to be
    (re)created` — one line, an @bail carries no cause; a @Context wrap adds
    `\n\nCaused by:\n    <cause @Display>`
  panic texts that can reach exit `101` through this file: `Failed building the Runtime`
    (macro @expect) and @std::@env::@args()'s invalid-unicode @panic! — none is written,
    only propagated, here
